== Ballot measures ==

=== Amendment 1 ===
The amendment increased the General Reserve Fund from 5% to 7% of state general fund revenues from the previous fiscal year. The increase was set to be phased in incrementally by one-half of one percent each year.
{{Infobox referendum|name=Amendment 1|location={{Flag|Tennessee}}|question=Must Section 36(A), Article III of the Constitution of this State, relating to the General Reserve Fund, be amended so as to provide that the General Reserve Fund of five percent of general fund revenue of the latest completed fiscal year must be increased each year by one-half of one percent of the general fund revenue of the latest completed fiscal year until it equals seven percent of such revenues?|yes=947,610|no=583,583|total=1,531,193|map=File:South Carolina Constitutional Amendment 1 results 2022.svg|mapcaption=County results<br>'''Yes'''
{{legend|#7D9CBB|60–70% |border=1px #AAAAAA solid}}
{{legend|#B6C8D9|50–60% |border=1px #AAAAAA solid}}|notes=Source: [[South Carolina Secretary of State]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=South Carolina Amendment 1, General Reserve Fund Increase Measure (2022) |url=https://ballotpedia.org/South_Carolina_Amendment_1,_General_Reserve_Fund_Increase_Measure_(2022) |access-date=2024-05-19 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref>|map_size=230px}}
{{Clear}}

=== Amendment 2 ===
The amendment increased the Capital Reserve Fund annual funding requirement from 2% to 3% of state general fund revenue.{{Infobox referendum|name=Amendment 2|location={{Flag|Tennessee}}|question=Must Section 36(B), Article III of the Constitution of this State be amended so as to provide that the Capital Reserve Fund of two percent of the general fund revenue of the latest completed fiscal year be increased to three percent of the general fund revenue of the latest completed fiscal year and to provide that the first use of the Capital Reserve Fund must be to offset midyear budget reductions?|yes=973,178|no=584,911|total=1,558,089|map=File:South Carolina Constitutional Amendment 2 results 2022.svg|mapcaption=County results<br>'''Yes'''
{{legend|#7D9CBB|60–70% |border=1px #AAAAAA solid}}
{{legend|#B6C8D9|50–60% |border=1px #AAAAAA solid}}|notes=Source: [[South Carolina Secretary of State]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=South Carolina Amendment 1, General Reserve Fund Increase Measure (2022) |url=https://ballotpedia.org/South_Carolina_Amendment_2,_Capital_Reserve_Fund_Increase_Measure_(2022) |access-date=2024-05-19 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref>|map_size=230px}}
